          "finish_reason": "length",
          "answer_excerpt": "# Technical Analysis of Plant Records: Reliability Assessment Report\n\n## 1. Executive Summary\n\nThis report analyzes 1,899 operational records spanning 10 equipment classes (pipeline, scrubber, turbine, pump, reactor, compressor, separator, heat exchanger, condenser, valve) across 400 stations. Each record contains five measured parameters: pressure (kPa), temperature (\u00b0C), flow (m\u00b3/h), vibration (mm/s), and efficiency (%).\n\nThe dataset exhibits a striking pattern: **vibration and efficiency are strongly inversely correlated**, and **high-vibration units cluster at low efficiency**. This is the signature of mechanical degradation \u2014 bearing wear, misalignment, impeller/blade erosion, and rotor imbalance \u2014 rather than process-side issues. A secondary pattern involves **sub-zero temperature readings** on units that should be operating hot, which points to instrumentation faults, cryogenic service, or severe flow starvation.\n\nThe most critical finding: **approximately 15\u201318% of all units are operating in a degraded or alarm state** (vibration > 7.5 mm/s combined with efficiency < 78%), and a subset of these show compound anomalies (extreme temperature, near-zero flow) that suggest imminent failure risk.\n\n---\n\n## ",
